# Lesson and reference prose

Follow the [Google developer documentation style guide](https://developers.google.com/style). Clarity, brevity, and inclusivity beat clever phrasing. Do not rewrite lessons 0001–0024 unless the user asks.

## Voice

- Conversational and direct. Sound like a knowledgeable colleague, not a pitch or a joke.
- Second person (`you`). Active voice. US English. Serial commas.
- Sentence case for titles and headings. Task headings start with a bare infinitive (`Configure a security context`). Concept headings are noun phrases (`Pod Security Standards`).
- Put the condition or goal before the instruction: "If the Pod is Running, run `kubectl exec`."
- Link text is the page title or a short description. Not `docs`, `here`, or a bare URL.
- Introduce a dedicated cross-reference with "For more information, see...".

## Clarity and brevity

- Prefer short sentences (under 26 words when you can). Lead with the point.
- Strong verbs. Avoid `there is` / `there are` openers, `simply`, `just`, `please`, and `easy`.
- Code in `code` font. Define an acronym on first use.

## Inclusivity

- No idioms, metaphors, humor, or culturally specific asides.
- No ableist or violent figurative language (`crazy`, `dummy`, `hang`, `hit`).
- Prefer `allowlist` / `blocklist`, `controller` / `replica`. If an API still uses a non-inclusive name, show it in code font once, then use the inclusive term.

```html
<!-- Not recommended -->
<p>The binding-scope trap. See <a href="...">[docs]</a>.</p>

<!-- Recommended -->
<p>A RoleBinding that references a ClusterRole still grants access only in that namespace. For more information, see <a href="https://kubernetes.io/docs/reference/access-authn-authz/rbac/">Using RBAC Authorization</a>.</p>
```
